# Open Cluster Management (OCM) – Installation, Managed Cluster Registration and Policy Add-ons

## 1. Environment

Example environment:

```bash
# Hub cluster context
export CTX_HUB_CLUSTER="kubernetes-admin@kubernetes"

# Managed cluster name in OCM
export MANAGED_CLUSTER_NAME="ocm-cluster1"

# Kubernetes context of the managed cluster
export CTX_MANAGED_CLUSTER="<managed-cluster-context>"
```

Check the clusteradm version:

```bash
clusteradm version
```

Expected:

```text
clusteradm      version :v1.3.1-0-g90bdc31
default bundle  version :1.3.1
```

---

# 2. Install/Initialize the OCM Hub

If OCM is not already installed on the hub:

```bash
clusteradm init \
  --wait \
  --context "${CTX_HUB_CLUSTER}"
```

If you want to pin the OCM bundle version:

```bash
clusteradm init \
  --bundle-version=1.3.1 \
  --wait \
  --context "${CTX_HUB_CLUSTER}"
```

OCM's `clusteradm init` installs the hub-side OCM control-plane components and generates the managed-cluster join command.

Verify:

```bash
kubectl get clustermanager \
  --context "${CTX_HUB_CLUSTER}"

kubectl get pods \
  -n open-cluster-management \
  --context "${CTX_HUB_CLUSTER}"

kubectl get pods \
  -n open-cluster-management-hub \
  --context "${CTX_HUB_CLUSTER}"
```

---

# 3. Get the Managed Cluster Join Command

On the hub:

```bash
clusteradm get token \
  --context "${CTX_HUB_CLUSTER}"
```

This generates the `clusteradm join` command containing the bootstrap token.

The generated command will look similar to:

```bash
clusteradm join \
  --hub-token <TOKEN> \
  --hub-apiserver https://<HUB-API-ENDPOINT>:6443 \
  --wait \
  --cluster-name "ocm-cluster1" \
  --context "${CTX_MANAGED_CLUSTER}"
```

The important requirement is that the hub API endpoint must be reachable from the managed cluster. OCM documents this as an outbound connection from the managed cluster to the hub API server.

For your environment, the hub endpoint was:

```text
https://192.168.2.10:6443
```

---

# 4. Join the Managed Cluster

Run the generated `clusteradm join` command against the managed-cluster context.

Example:

```bash
clusteradm join \
  --hub-token <TOKEN> \
  --hub-apiserver https://192.168.2.10:6443 \
  --wait \
  --cluster-name "ocm-cluster1" \
  --context "${CTX_MANAGED_CLUSTER}"
```

Do NOT use `--force-internal-endpoint-lookup` unless your deployment specifically requires internal endpoint resolution. For a normal managed cluster that can reach the hub API endpoint, use the network-accessible hub address.

Verify the OCM agents on the managed cluster:

```bash
kubectl get pods \
  -n open-cluster-management-agent \
  --context "${CTX_MANAGED_CLUSTER}"
```

Expected:

```text
klusterlet-registration-agent-xxxxx    1/1   Running
klusterlet-work-agent-xxxxx            1/1   Running
```

---

# 5. Accept the Managed Cluster on the Hub

On the hub:

```bash
clusteradm accept \
  --clusters "${MANAGED_CLUSTER_NAME}" \
  --context "${CTX_HUB_CLUSTER}"
```

The `accept` operation approves the registration and creates the required managed-cluster namespace/RBAC resources on the hub.

Verify:

```bash
kubectl get managedcluster \
  "${MANAGED_CLUSTER_NAME}" \
  --context "${CTX_HUB_CLUSTER}"
```

Expected:

```text
NAME           HUB ACCEPTED   MANAGED CLUSTER URLS   JOINED   AVAILABLE
ocm-cluster1   true            ...                    True     True
```

Also verify:

```bash
kubectl get managedcluster -o wide \
  --context "${CTX_HUB_CLUSTER}"
```

---

# 6. Verify the Managed Cluster Namespace

On the hub:

```bash
kubectl get ns "${MANAGED_CLUSTER_NAME}" \
  --context "${CTX_HUB_CLUSTER}"
```

Expected:

```text
NAME           STATUS   AGE
ocm-cluster1   Active   ...
```

This namespace is important because OCM uses the managed-cluster namespace to hold resources such as:

* ManagedClusterAddOn
* ManifestWork
* RBAC resources
* other cluster-specific OCM objects

---

# 7. Install Governance Policy Framework on the Hub

Install the governance policy framework hub components:

```bash
clusteradm install hub-addon \
  --names governance-policy-framework \
  --context "${CTX_HUB_CLUSTER}"
```

Verify:

```bash
kubectl get deployments \
  -n open-cluster-management \
  --context "${CTX_HUB_CLUSTER}" | \
grep -E 'governance-policy|policy-propagator'
```

Expected components include:

```text
governance-policy-addon-controller
governance-policy-propagator
```

Verify pods:

```bash
kubectl get pods \
  -n open-cluster-management \
  --context "${CTX_HUB_CLUSTER}" | \
grep -E 'governance-policy|policy-propagator'
```

OCM documents `clusteradm install hub-addon --names governance-policy-framework` for installing the policy framework controllers on the hub.

---

# 8. Enable Governance Policy Framework on the Managed Cluster

From the hub:

```bash
clusteradm addon enable \
  --names governance-policy-framework \
  --clusters "${MANAGED_CLUSTER_NAME}" \
  --context "${CTX_HUB_CLUSTER}"
```

Verify:

```bash
kubectl get managedclusteraddon \
  -n "${MANAGED_CLUSTER_NAME}" \
  governance-policy-framework \
  -o wide \
  --context "${CTX_HUB_CLUSTER}"
```

Expected:

```text
NAME                          AVAILABLE   DEGRADED   PROGRESSING
governance-policy-framework   True                    False
```

