# 13. Create/Verify a Placement

For a policy to be delivered to a managed cluster, use a Placement and PlacementBinding.

Example:

```bash
kubectl get placement -A \
  --context "${CTX_HUB_CLUSTER}"

kubectl get placementdecision -A \
  --context "${CTX_HUB_CLUSTER}"

kubectl get placementbinding -A \
  --context "${CTX_HUB_CLUSTER}"
```

Verify that the Placement selects:

```text
ocm-cluster1
```

---

# 14. Verify OCM Policy Resources

IMPORTANT:

Do not rely on:

```bash
kubectl get policy
```

on this environment because there are multiple policy APIs/resources, including Kyverno.

Use the full OCM resource:

```bash
kubectl get policies.policy.open-cluster-management.io -A \
  --context "${CTX_HUB_CLUSTER}"
```

For a specific policy:

```bash
kubectl get policies.policy.open-cluster-management.io \
  -n default \
  autosys-resource-reader-policy \
  -o yaml \
  --context "${CTX_HUB_CLUSTER}"
```

---

# 15. Verify the Replicated Policy

The root policy is normally in its original namespace, while the replicated policy appears in the managed-cluster namespace.

Check:

```bash
kubectl get policies.policy.open-cluster-management.io \
  -n "${MANAGED_CLUSTER_NAME}" \
  default.autosys-resource-reader-policy \
  -o jsonpath='{.status}' | jq \
  --context "${CTX_HUB_CLUSTER}"
```

Expected:

```json
{
  "compliant": "Compliant"
}
```

Check the root policy:

```bash
kubectl get policies.policy.open-cluster-management.io \
  -n default \
  autosys-resource-reader-policy \
  -o jsonpath='{.status}' | jq \
  --context "${CTX_HUB_CLUSTER}"
```

Expected:

```json
{
  "compliant": "Compliant",
  "status": [
    {
      "clustername": "ocm-cluster1",
      "clusternamespace": "ocm-cluster1",
      "compliant": "Compliant"
    }
  ]
}
```

